      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When your LDL levels are high, the cholesterol molecules penetrate the inner lining of your arteries, specifically the endothelium. Once there, they trigger an inflammatory response where immune cells, particularly macrophages, migrate to the site and engulf the oxidized cholesterol. These macrophages transform into \"foam cells,\" which accumulate and form a fatty streak. Over time, these streaks develop into atherosclerotic plaques.\n\nThe plaque causes problems through two main mechanisms: narrowing and instability. First, as the plaque grows, it physically narrows the lumen of the artery, reducing blood flow to vital organs like the heart or brain. This can lead to angina (chest pain) or ischemic stroke. Second, and perhaps more dangerously, the plaque cap can become unstable and rupture. When this happens, the body responds by forming a blood clot at the rupture site. This clot can suddenly and completely block the artery, causing a heart attack or a massive stroke. Additionally, the inflammatory process within the plaque itself can damage the vessel wall, making it prone to tearing.\n\nSo, it is not just about a simple blockage; it is a complex, progressive disease involving inflammation, cellular transformation, and the risk of sudden",
